---
import {THRESHOLD, WEIGHT} from '@atlas/shared/consensus'
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import {site} from '../config/site'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How it works"
  description="How a claim becomes an answer on the Faux Pas Atlas, and why no editor ever types a verdict."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-narrow prose">
      <h1 class="t-h1">Nobody here is an authority</h1>

      <p class="t-lead">
        Every other etiquette guide is one writer with a deadline. This one has
        no verdict field. Ask the database what it thinks is rude in Lisbon and
        it cannot tell you, because the answer is not stored anywhere. It is
        computed, every time, from what people who live there have said.
      </p>

      <h2 class="t-h2">A claim is not an answer</h2>
      <p>
        A claim is one behaviour, in one place, in one situation. "Tipping at a
        restaurant" in Japan, at dinner. That is all the document holds:
        the statement, the two references, and its stage. No stance, no score,
        no summary.
      </p>

      <h2 class="t-h2">Voices, weighted</h2>
      <p>
        Anyone can answer rude, fine or depends. Not every answer counts the
        same. Someone who says they are from there counts {WEIGHT.local}×,
        someone who lived there {WEIGHT['lived-there']}×, a visitor {WEIGHT.visitor}×.
        Confidence nudges it a little either way. We take people at their word
        about where they are from, which is the weakest joint in the whole
        design, and the reason every claim shows its split rather than just its
        winner.
      </p>

      <h2 class="t-h2">When it becomes settled</h2>
      <p>
        A claim needs {THRESHOLD.minAttestations} attestations, at least
        {THRESHOLD.minLocals} of them from locals, and {Math.round(THRESHOLD.minAgreement * 100)}%
        of the weighted voices pointing the same way. Then it reads as settled.
        If agreement falls below {Math.round(THRESHOLD.contestedBelow * 100)}% it
        moves to the contested board instead, where it stays visible with the
        disagreement on display. There is no state in which this site prints a
        confident answer that the locals do not actually share.
      </p>

      <h2 class="t-h2">Nothing is final</h2>
      <p>
        Manners drift. A settled claim that nobody has attested to in a year
        reopens by itself and has to earn its place again. Anyone can dispute
        one in writing, which flags it for a moderator, who can keep it, split
        it into narrower claims, or retire it. Retired claims stay in the
        database with their whole history; they just stop being shown.
      </p>

      <h2 class="t-h2">How it is built</h2>
      <p>
        Content lives in Sanity. The stages are a workflow, the recomputation
        and the duplicate check are serverless functions, and moderators rule on
        disputes in a custom app that sits next to the editor. The site itself
        is Astro, served as static files with a few live parts.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small t-dim">
        The dataset is public: project <code>{site.projectId}</code>, dataset
        <code>{site.dataset}</code>. Every number on this site can be recomputed
        from it by anyone who wants to check our arithmetic.
      </p>

      <div class="button-row cta">
        <a class="button" href="/atlas/">Browse the atlas</a>
        <a class="button button-outline" href="/propose/">Add a claim</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: 24px;
  }

  .prose p {
    margin: 0;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .prose code {
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--paper-2);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85em;
  }

  .cta {
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
</style>
